1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,201
This is PhishGuard, an AI-based
phishing website detection

2
00:00:03,251 --> 00:00:07,168
and risk analysis system by Ayushi
Tiwari, guided by Mr. Vishal Gupta.

3
00:00:08,101 --> 00:00:10,827
Phishing sites copy trusted
brands to steal passwords,

4
00:00:10,877 --> 00:00:13,140
and many vanish before
blocklists catch them.

5
00:00:13,190 --> 00:00:15,248
PhishGuard explains the risk of any link.

6
00:00:15,802 --> 00:00:19,720
Each URL becomes 44 features
for a Random Forest model,

7
00:00:19,770 --> 00:00:24,570
while live checks inspect DNS, WHOIS,
SSL, content, brands and threat feeds.

8
00:00:25,122 --> 00:00:27,252
The Overview dashboard shows total scans,

9
00:00:27,302 --> 00:00:31,133
detection rate and average trust score,
with trends and a live scan feed.

10
00:00:32,124 --> 00:00:34,759
Let's deep scan a fake PayPal login link.

11
00:00:34,809 --> 00:00:39,737
Eight stages run, from the ML prediction
to DNS, WHOIS, SSL and reputation.

12
00:00:42,126 --> 00:00:45,134
The verdict is phishing: risk 92 out of 100,

13
00:00:45,184 --> 00:00:48,613
trust score 8, tier Untrusted,
threat type social engineering.

14
00:00:49,165 --> 00:00:52,658
Every verdict is explained: an AI
summary, the factors behind it,

15
00:00:52,708 --> 00:00:55,329
and a 60/40 split between model and checks.

16
00:00:56,167 --> 00:00:59,167
ML Explanation shows the
features behind the prediction,

17
00:00:59,217 --> 00:01:01,890
like the long hostname,
and all four models agree.

18
00:01:01,940 --> 00:01:03,851
Brand confirms PayPal impersonation.

19
00:01:04,402 --> 00:01:08,456
Next, a live phishing kit on
vercel.app: WHOIS and DNS records,

20
00:01:08,506 --> 00:01:11,655
a mismatched SSL certificate,
and its redirect chain.

21
00:01:12,405 --> 00:01:15,275
Reputation shows 3 of
11 engines flagging it,

22
00:01:15,325 --> 00:01:17,368
including OpenPhish and Phishing Army.

23
00:01:17,418 --> 00:01:19,461
Content classifies it as a login page.

24
00:01:20,013 --> 00:01:23,220
Resources lists contacted
domains, Source keeps the HTML,

25
00:01:23,270 --> 00:01:28,021
which hides code sending stolen data to
Telegram, and Graph maps the infrastructure.

26
00:01:28,572 --> 00:01:31,768
For contrast, github.com
is legitimate: risk 3,

27
00:01:31,818 --> 00:01:35,487
trust score 97, tier Trusted,
and no engine flags it.

28
00:01:36,038 --> 00:01:40,574
History keeps every scan with export and
one-click pivots, like all Untrusted sites.

29
00:01:40,624 --> 00:01:42,812
Bulk Scan checks up to 500 URLs.

30
00:01:44,042 --> 00:01:47,443
Analytics shows scan trends, an
activity heatmap, threat types,

31
00:01:47,493 --> 00:01:50,730
trust tiers and a detection
funnel from scanned to phishing.

32
00:01:51,282 --> 00:01:55,753
The Model Lab compares four
classifiers on 146,000 URLs.

33
00:01:55,803 --> 00:02:00,446
Random Forest wins on F1, with 86%
accuracy even on unseen domains,

34
00:02:00,496 --> 00:02:02,564
and an AUC of 0.94.

35
00:02:03,283 --> 00:02:07,341
The Relationship graph links domains
by shared IP addresses and registrars.

36
00:02:07,391 --> 00:02:09,531
Watchlist re-checks sites automatically,

37
00:02:09,581 --> 00:02:11,941
and Typosquat finds live PayPal look-alikes.

38
00:02:12,495 --> 00:02:15,405
Community reports work like
PhishTank, with crowd voting,

39
00:02:15,455 --> 00:02:19,248
and Threat Feeds holds
657,000 blocklist entries.

40
00:02:19,802 --> 00:02:23,859
Alerts flag high-risk detections, the
Activity log records every action,

41
00:02:23,909 --> 00:02:27,567
Settings tunes the thresholds,
and a REST API exposes everything.

42
00:02:28,120 --> 00:02:31,199
Under the hood: a React
dashboard, a FastAPI backend

43
00:02:31,249 --> 00:02:35,213
with the model and analysis engine,
and SQLite storage, on Vercel.

44
00:02:35,767 --> 00:02:38,760
PhishGuard turns any link into
an explainable risk score.

45
00:02:38,810 --> 00:02:41,056
The live demo and code links are on screen.

46
00:02:41,106 --> 00:02:41,590
Thank you.
